<DocsLayout
  title="Privacy Policy"
  description="How ClipCut handles media, transcripts, AI API keys, and optional account data."
  currentPath="/privacy"
  section="Legal"
>
  <h2>1. Local-First by Design</h2>
  <p>
    ClipCut runs on your own machine or server. Videos, downloaded streams, rendered clips,
    transcripts, and SQLite/PostgreSQL databases stay in your configured storage directory (
    <code>CLIPCUT_DATA</code>) and are never sent to ClipCut authors.
  </p>

  <h2>2. AI Providers &amp; Speech Processing</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Local Models:</strong> Speech-to-text via <code>sherpa-onnx</code> and{" "}
      <code>faster-whisper</code>, as well as LLM inference via <code>ollama</code>, run 100%
      locally without external network calls.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Cloud AI Keys:</strong> If you configure a cloud AI key in{" "}
      <strong>Settings → AI</strong> or <code>.env</code>, transcripts and prompt context are sent
      directly from your server to the provider you chose.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>3. Connected Social Accounts &amp; Email</h2>
  <p>
    OAuth tokens for YouTube, Instagram, and TikTok, as well as optional account emails (when{" "}
    <code>CLIPCUT_ACCOUNTS=1</code>), are stored solely in your own instance database to perform
    actions you explicitly request.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
